.TH "FLUX-SCHEDBENCH" "1" "Jun 17, 2026" "" "flux-schedbench"
.SH NAME
flux-schedbench \- run benchmarks against fake or real resources
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
\fBflux\fP \fBschedbench\fP \fBrun\fP \fITEST\fP [\fIOPTIONS\fP]
\fBflux\fP \fBschedbench\fP \fBsweep\fP [\fITEST\fP] [\fIOPTIONS\fP]
\fBflux\fP \fBschedbench\fP \fBreport\fP \fITEST\fP [\fIOPTIONS\fP]
.fi
.sp
.SH DESCRIPTION
.sp
\fBflux schedbench\fP runs scheduler benchmarks against fake or real
resources and saves the results for later reporting.
.sp
By default, \fBflux schedbench run\fP launches a fresh Flux subinstance
configured with fake resources via the fake\-resources modprobe rc1 task
(see \X'tty: link https://flux-framework.readthedocs.io/projects/flux-core/en/latest/man5/flux-config-fake-resources.html'\fI\%flux\-config\-fake\-resources(5)\fP\X'tty: link') and re\-execs itself inside that
subinstance to run the benchmark. With \fB\-\-exec\fP, no subinstance is
launched and real jobs run against the broker the user is currently
connected to.
.sp
\fBflux schedbench sweep\fP runs a cross\-product of benchmark
configurations as parallel Flux jobs against the outer instance, sharing
one results file and one live dashboard.  Single\-value parameters stay
fixed across the sweep; comma lists and RFC 45 ranges become sweep axes.
Use \fB\-\-from FILE.toml\fP for structured definitions including multi\-module
scheduler recipes.
.SH COMMANDS
.SS run
.sp
\fBflux schedbench run\fP runs a named benchmark (see \fI\%BENCHMARKS\fP
below) and appends the result to the results file.
.INDENT 0.0
.TP
.B \-N, \-\-nodes=N
Fake\-resource node count for the launched subinstance (default: 4).
Ignored with \fI\%\-\-exec\fP — the real broker\(aqs resources are used
instead.
.UNINDENT
.INDENT 0.0
.TP
.B \-c, \-\-cores\-per\-node=C
Cores per node in the fake resource set (default: 64). Ignored with
\fI\%\-\-exec\fP\&.
.UNINDENT
.INDENT 0.0
.TP
.B \-g, \-\-gpus\-per\-node=G
GPUs per node in the fake resource set (default: 8). Ignored with
\fI\%\-\-exec\fP\&.
.UNINDENT
.INDENT 0.0
.TP
.B \-\-hwloc\-xml\-path=PATH
Path to an hwloc XML file describing per\-node topology. Passed to
the subinstance as \fB\-\-conf=fake\-resources.hwloc\-xml\-path=PATH\fP;
the XML\(aqs per\-node shape is replicated across
\fI\%\-\-nodes\fP\&. Useful for benchmarking topology\-aware schedulers
(e.g. Fluxion). The path is also recorded in the results file so
reports can show which run used which topology. Ignored with
\fI\%\-\-exec\fP\&.
.UNINDENT
.INDENT 0.0
.TP
.B \-\-amend\-r=SPEC
Reference to a Python amender that mutates R before it is written
to KVS. \fBSPEC\fP is either a \fBmodule:function\fP reference or a
path to a file with an \fBamend()\fP callable at module scope; see
\X'tty: link https://flux-framework.readthedocs.io/projects/flux-core/en/latest/man5/flux-config-fake-resources.html'\fI\%flux\-config\-fake\-resources(5)\fP\X'tty: link' for the amender contract.
Passed to the subinstance as
\fB\-\-conf=fake\-resources.amend\-r=SPEC\fP\&. Typically paired with
\fI\%\-\-hwloc\-xml\-path\fP to inject scheduler\-specific topology
metadata (Fluxion JGF, node properties, etc.) into R. The spec
is recorded in the results file. Ignored with \fI\%\-\-exec\fP\&.
.UNINDENT
.INDENT 0.0
.TP
.B \-\-scheduler=NAME
Scheduler module to load in the fake\-resources subinstance (default:
\fBsched\-simple\fP). Implemented by passing
\fB\-\-conf=modules.alternatives.sched=NAME\fP to the underlying
\X'tty: link https://flux-framework.readthedocs.io/projects/flux-core/en/latest/man1/flux-start.html'\fI\%flux\-start(1)\fP\X'tty: link'\&. Ignored with \fI\%\-\-exec\fP\&.
.UNINDENT
.INDENT 0.0
.TP
.B \-\-scheduler\-options=OPTS
Module options string for the scheduler, shlex\-parsed into a list and
encoded into the subinstance config as
\fB\-\-conf=modules.sched.args=[\(dqopt1\(dq, \(dqopt2\(dq]\fP\&. Ignored with
\fI\%\-\-exec\fP\&.
.UNINDENT
.INDENT 0.0
.TP
.B \-o, \-\-extra\-start\-options=OPTS
Extra arguments to pass through to the underlying \X'tty: link https://flux-framework.readthedocs.io/projects/flux-core/en/latest/man1/flux-start.html'\fI\%flux\-start(1)\fP\X'tty: link'
when launching the fake\-resources subinstance; shlex\-parsed. May be
given multiple times. Useful for setting broker attributes
(\fB\-\-setattr=...\fP) or other conf keys that schedbench doesn\(aqt have
a dedicated flag for. Ignored with \fI\%\-\-exec\fP\&.
.UNINDENT
.INDENT 0.0
.TP
.B \-x, \-\-exec
Run real jobs against the current enclosing instance — no fake
resources, no subinstance launch. Use when running inside an
allocation where the broker\(aqs currently\-loaded scheduler and
currently\-up resources are what you want to benchmark.
.UNINDENT
.INDENT 0.0
.TP
.B \-\-watcher=NAME
Event\-watcher implementation (default: \fBjournal\fP). The journal
watcher imposes a single subscription on kvs\-watch; the per\-job watcher
opens one subscription per job, useful for measuring watcher overhead
under high job counts. Valid values are \fBjournal\fP and \fBper\-job\fP\&.
.UNINDENT
.INDENT 0.0
.TP
.B \-n, \-\-njobs=N
Number of jobs to submit (default: 1000). Used by throughput and
locality; ignored by fill\-machine, which computes its job count
from the resource shape.
.UNINDENT
.INDENT 0.0
.TP
.B \-\-slot\-cores=K
Cores per slot (default: 1). The total cores requested by each
job is \fB\-\-slot\-cores * \-\-nslots\fP\&.
.UNINDENT
.INDENT 0.0
.TP
.B \-\-slot\-gpus=K
GPUs per slot (default: 0). The total GPUs requested by each job
is \fB\-\-slot\-gpus * \-\-nslots\fP\&.
.UNINDENT
.INDENT 0.0
.TP
.B \-\-nslots=N
Slots per job (locality only; default: 1). Each slot requests
\fI\%\-\-slot\-cores\fP cores and \fI\%\-\-slot\-gpus\fP GPUs;
locality scores how well the scheduler packs each slot into a
single locality domain.
.UNINDENT
.INDENT 0.0
.TP
.B \-\-duration=SPEC
Job duration (locality only; default: \fB0.1s\fP). \fBSPEC\fP is one
of:
.INDENT 7.0
.IP \(bu 2
an FSD string (e.g. \fB0.1s\fP, \fB2m\fP) — every job runs that
long;
.IP \(bu 2
\fBfill\fP — jobs never complete; the benchmark saturates the
cluster and cancels everything once placement is observed;
.IP \(bu 2
\fBrandom[:LO\-HI]\fP — uniform random duration per job (default
range \fB0.1s\-1.0s\fP).
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.INDENT 0.0
.TP
.B \-\-tag=LABEL
Free\-form label stored in the results metadata for filtering and
reporting.
.UNINDENT
.INDENT 0.0
.TP
.B \-\-results\-file=PATH
Results file path (default: \fB\&./schedbench\-results.json\fP). The file
is created if absent; new runs append to the existing record list.
.UNINDENT
.INDENT 0.0
.TP
.B \-\-no\-save
Don\(aqt append to the results file. Useful for ad\-hoc runs that
shouldn\(aqt pollute the persistent record.
.UNINDENT
.INDENT 0.0
.TP
.B \-\-ui=auto|on|off
Interactive terminal UI: \fBauto\fP (default) enables it when stdout is a
TTY and \fI\%\-\-quiet\fP is not set; \fBon\fP forces it; \fBoff\fP falls
back to the JSON event stream.
.UNINDENT
.INDENT 0.0
.TP
.B \-\-color=auto|always|never
Color output for the terminal UI: \fBauto\fP (default) honors NO_COLOR and
TERM; \fBalways\fP / \fBnever\fP force.
.UNINDENT
.INDENT 0.0
.TP
.B \-q, \-\-quiet
Emit only terminal events (\fBtest.start\fP, \fBresult\fP,
\fBtest.complete\fP, \fBtest.error\fP). On a non\-TTY stdout, produces a
single JSON object containing the result metrics.
.UNINDENT
.INDENT 0.0
.TP
.B \-v, \-\-verbose
Emit progress, info, and metric events from the benchmark.
.UNINDENT
.SS report
.sp
\fBflux schedbench report\fP pretty\-prints results for a single
benchmark from the results file.
.INDENT 0.0
.TP
.B \-\-results\-file=PATH
Results file path (default: \fB\&./schedbench\-results.json\fP).
.UNINDENT
.INDENT 0.0
.TP
.B \-\-filter=KEY=VAL
Show only runs matching \fIKEY=VAL\fP\&. May be given multiple times;
filters combine with AND semantics. Common keys: \fBtag\fP, \fBwatcher\fP,
\fBscheduler.name\fP, \fBreal_exec\fP\&.
.UNINDENT
.INDENT 0.0
.TP
.B \-o, \-\-format=FORMAT
Output format. May be a named format (\fBdefault\fP, \fBlong\fP, \fBcsv\fP,
or any user\-defined format from
\fB~/.config/flux/flux\-schedbench\-report\-TEST.toml\fP; pass \fBhelp\fP to
list available names), or a literal Python\-style format string.
.UNINDENT
.INDENT 0.0
.TP
.B \-\-sort=KEY,...
Sort rows by one or more keys. Prefix a key with \fB\-\fP for descending
order.
.UNINDENT
.INDENT 0.0
.TP
.B \-\-no\-header
Omit the header row.
.UNINDENT
.SS sweep
.sp
\fBflux schedbench sweep\fP runs a cross\-product of benchmark
configurations and writes all results to a single results file with
shared sweep metadata.  Each point in the matrix becomes one child
\fBflux schedbench run\fP invocation submitted as a Flux job to the
outer instance; the dispatcher tracks them in parallel through a single
\fBflux.job.JobWatcher\fP and renders progress to a live dashboard.
.sp
Sweep flags mirror \fBflux schedbench run\fP (same names, same
semantics), with one extension: every axis\-capable flag accepts either a
single value (fixed across the sweep) or a multi\-value spec that becomes
a sweep axis.  Multi\-value specs are:
.INDENT 0.0
.IP \(bu 2
a comma list — \fB\-\-nodes=4,8,16\fP
.IP \(bu 2
an RFC 45 range — \fB\-\-nodes=4\-1024:2:*\fP (4 to 1024, step ×2)
.IP \(bu 2
a Python list in TOML — \fBnjobs = [4096, 8192]\fP
.UNINDENT
.sp
The axis\-capable flags are: \fB\-\-nodes\fP, \fB\-\-cores\-per\-node\fP,
\fB\-\-gpus\-per\-node\fP, \fB\-\-njobs\fP, \fB\-\-slot\-cores\fP,
\fB\-\-slot\-gpus\fP, \fB\-\-hwloc\-xml\-path\fP, \fB\-\-amend\-r\fP,
\fB\-\-scheduler\fP, \fB\-\-scheduler\-options\fP, \fB\-\-watcher\fP,
\fB\-\-tag\fP, and the \fITEST\fP positional itself (which accepts a comma
list for a multi\-benchmark sweep).
.sp
The sweep itself runs against the outer Flux instance.  To run a sweep
without a pre\-existing outer instance, wrap in \X'tty: link https://flux-framework.readthedocs.io/projects/flux-core/en/latest/man1/flux-start.html'\fI\%flux\-start(1)\fP\X'tty: link':
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
flux start \-s 1 \-\- flux schedbench sweep ...
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
Sweep\-only flags:
.INDENT 0.0
.TP
.B \-\-from=PATH
Load sweep parameters and scheduler recipes from a TOML file.  CLI
flag values for the same parameters take precedence over file values.
See \fI\%SWEEP TOML FORMAT\fP below.
.UNINDENT
.INDENT 0.0
.TP
.B \-\-sweep\-name=NAME
Human\-readable label for this sweep, stored in every record\(aqs
\fBsweep.name\fP field.  Default: a timestamp\-based name.
.UNINDENT
.INDENT 0.0
.TP
.B \-\-per\-run\-nodes=N
Nodes to allocate to each child Flux job (default: 1).  The child
schedbench process spawns a fake\-resources subinstance inside its
allocation, so for fake\-resources benchmarks one outer node per child
is sufficient regardless of the per\-run \fB\-\-nodes\fP value.
.UNINDENT
.sp
The standard \fBrun\fP\-side flags also apply (\fB\-\-results\-file\fP,
\fB\-\-no\-save\fP, \fB\-\-ui\fP, \fB\-\-color\fP, \fB\-\-conf\fP,
\fB\-\-real\-exec\fP).
.SH SWEEP TOML FORMAT
.sp
A sweep definition file is a TOML document; the whole file is the sweep
(no outer \fB[sweep]\fP wrapper).  Top\-level scalars become fixed
parameters; top\-level lists and RFC 45 strings become sweep axes.
Structured sections (\fB[[scheduler]]\fP, \fB[conf]\fP) configure dispatch.
.sp
A complete example, using Fluxion from a build directory:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
# Optional sweep label.  Auto\-generated if omitted.
name = \(dqfluxion\-vs\-simple\(dq

# Top\-level parameters.  Single values are fixed across the
# sweep; lists and RFC 45 ranges become axes.
test = \(dqthroughput\(dq
nodes = \(dq16\-1024:2\(dq            # axis: 16, 32, 64, ..., 1024
njobs = [4096, 8192]           # axis: explicit list
cores_per_node = 64            # fixed: scalar
real_exec = false              # fixed: scalar

# Conf entries forwarded to every child run as \-\-conf=KEY=VALUE.
# Equivalent to passing \-\-conf=KEY=VALUE on the sweep command line.
[conf]
\(dqresource.noverify\(dq = true

# [[scheduler]] array\-of\-tables: each entry contributes one value
# to the implicit \(dqscheduler\(dq axis.  \(ga\(ganame\(ga\(ga is the axis label and
# appears in records / dashboard / report rows.
[[scheduler]]
name = \(dqsimple\(dq

[[scheduler]]
name = \(dqfluxion\(dq
env = [
  \(dqFLUXION_HOME=/home/user/git/flux\-sched\(dq,
  \(dqFLUX_MODPROBE_PATH_APPEND=$FLUXION_HOME/etc/modprobe\(dq,
  \(dqFLUX_PYTHONPATH_PREPEND=$FLUXION_HOME/src/python\(dq,
  \(dqFLUX_MODULE_PATH_PREPEND=$FLUXION_HOME/resource/modules:$FLUXION_HOME/qmanager/modules\(dq,
]

[[scheduler.modules]]
name = \(dqsched\-fluxion\-resource\(dq
options = \(dqpolicy=high\(dq

[[scheduler.modules]]
name = \(dqsched\-fluxion\-qmanager\(dq
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
Scheduler recipe fields:
.INDENT 0.0
.TP
.B \fBname\fP (required)
Axis value used in records, the dashboard, and report rows.  Also
the default \fBmodule\fP to force\-load when no \fBmodules\fP block is
given (so a shorthand block with just \fBname = \(dqsched\-backfill\(dq\fP
means \(dqforce\-load sched\-backfill\(dq).
.TP
.B \fBmodule\fP (optional)
Scheduler module name to force\-load via
\fB\-\-conf=modules.alternatives.sched=NAME\fP\&.  Defaults to \fBname\fP
in shorthand\-form blocks (just \fBname\fP, no \fBmodules\fP).  When
\fBmodules\fP is present, \fBmodule\fP defaults to \fBNone\fP — no
\fB\-\-scheduler\fP override, modprobe picks the default via priority
resolution.
.TP
.B \fBmodules\fP (optional)
List of \fB{name, options}\fP tables.  Each entry\(aqs \fBoptions\fP (if
present) is encoded as \fB\-\-conf=modules.<name>.args=[...]\fP for the
child run.  Order is informational; modprobe decides load order
from dependencies.
.TP
.B \fBconf\fP (optional)
Table of additional \fB\-\-conf=KEY=VALUE\fP entries applied only when
this recipe is selected (in addition to the sweep\(aqs top\-level
\fB[conf]\fP).  Useful for scheduler\-specific tuning.
.TP
.B \fBenv\fP (optional)
List of \fBflux.job.JobspecV1.from_submit\fP\-style env filter
rules applied to the child job\(aqs environment.  Each rule is one of:
.INDENT 7.0
.IP \(bu 2
\fB\(dqVAR=VALUE\(dq\fP — set; \fB$VAR\fP / \fB${VAR}\fP expand against the
env built by earlier rules
.IP \(bu 2
\fB\(dqVAR\(dq\fP or \fB\(dqGLOB*\(dq\fP — import from the submitter env
.IP \(bu 2
\fB\(dq\-PATTERN\(dq\fP — delete matching variables
.IP \(bu 2
\fB\(dq^/path\(dq\fP — read additional rules from a file
.UNINDENT
.sp
The submitter environment is preserved by default; rules only add
or remove.  As a convenience, a dict \fBenv = { K = \(dqV\(dq, ... }\fP is
accepted and converted to \fB[\(dqK=V\(dq, ...]\fP rules — handy for the
simple \(dqset these vars\(dq case without inter\-rule \fB$VAR\fP
references.
.UNINDENT
.sp
Common TOML pitfall — keys placed after sub\-table headers attach to the
\fIsub\-table\fP, not the parent.  Place top\-level keys (\fBenv\fP, \fBconf\fP)
\fIbefore\fP any \fB[[scheduler.modules]]\fP headers, since headers terminate
the current scope:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
# CORRECT
[[scheduler]]
name = \(dqfluxion\(dq
env = [...]              # belongs to [[scheduler]]

[[scheduler.modules]]
name = \(dqsched\-fluxion\-resource\(dq

# WRONG — env attaches to the module entry, not the scheduler
[[scheduler]]
name = \(dqfluxion\(dq

[[scheduler.modules]]
name = \(dqsched\-fluxion\-resource\(dq
env = [...]              # attaches to the module — error
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
The TOML parser raises a hard error for unknown keys in
\fB[[scheduler]]\fP and \fB[[scheduler.modules]]\fP blocks to surface this
errors immediately rather than silently dropping the misplaced key.
.SH BENCHMARKS
.INDENT 0.0
.TP
.B throughput
Submit \fIN\fP jobs as fast as possible and measure submit, alloc,
ingest, and clean rates. Use \fB\-\-njobs\fP to control the job
count and \fB\-\-slot\-cores\fP / \fB\-\-slot\-gpus\fP to
control per\-job resource requirements. Headline metric:
\fBthroughput\fP (jobs/sec, broker\-side from submit to clean).
.TP
.B fill\-machine
Submit jobs sized to saturate the resource set; measure
time\-to\-fill, then cancel and measure cleanup. Use
\fB\-\-slot\-cores\fP and \fB\-\-slot\-gpus\fP to control
per\-job requirements; the job count is computed from the
resource shape. Headline metric: \fBtime_to_fill\fP (seconds from
first submit to last \fBstart\fP event).
.TP
.B locality
Submit multi\-slot jobs and score each placement against an hwloc
topology. A slot is \(dqsatisfied\(dq when its cores and GPUs all
share a single locality domain (by default the NUMA node
identified by hwloc\(aqs \fBnodeset\fP attribute). Use
\fB\-\-nslots\fP, \fB\-\-slot\-cores\fP, and
\fB\-\-slot\-gpus\fP to shape the request, and
\fB\-\-duration\fP to control job lifetime. Requires
\fB\-\-hwloc\-xml\-path\fP — the predicate needs the topology to
score against. Headline metric: \fBmean_locality_score\fP (mean
per\-job satisfied / requested slots, in \fB[0, 1]\fP).
.UNINDENT
.SH EXAMPLES
.sp
Run a basic throughput benchmark against a 4\-node fake cluster (the
defaults), saving to the default results file:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
$ flux schedbench run throughput \-\-njobs=500
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
Benchmark against a larger fake cluster and tag the run:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
$ flux schedbench run throughput \-N 100 \-\-cores\-per\-node=32 \e
    \-\-njobs=10000 \-\-tag=large\-throughput
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
Benchmark against an alternative scheduler:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
$ flux schedbench run throughput \-N 100 \e
    \-\-scheduler=sched\-fluxion\-qmanager \e
    \-\-scheduler\-options=\(dqqueue\-depth=64\(dq
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
Benchmark Fluxion with topology\-aware scheduling against a synthetic 100\-node
cluster, using the local machine\(aqs hwloc topology replicated per node and a
Fluxion JGF amender to populate \fBR[\(dqscheduling\(dq]\fP:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
$ lstopo \-\-of xml local.xml
$ flux schedbench run throughput \-N 100 \e
    \-\-hwloc\-xml\-path=./local.xml \e
    \-\-amend\-r=./fluxion_amender.py \e
    \-\-scheduler=sched\-fluxion\-qmanager
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
Measure placement quality on a synthetic NUMA\-rich topology — how well does
the scheduler keep each 4\-core slot inside a single NUMA domain?:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
$ lstopo \-i \(dqpackage:2 numa:4 core:8 pu:1\(dq \-\-of xml > syn.xml
$ flux schedbench run locality \-N 16 \-\-hwloc\-xml\-path=./syn.xml \e
    \-\-nslots=2 \-\-slot\-cores=4 \-\-njobs=200
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
Compare locality and throughput trade\-offs across schedulers by running both
benchmarks against each and reading the headline metrics from the same
results file:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
$ for sched in sched\-simple sched\-fluxion\-qmanager; do
>     flux schedbench run locality \-N 16 \e
>         \-\-hwloc\-xml\-path=./syn.xml \-\-scheduler=$sched \e
>         \-\-tag=$sched \-\-nslots=2 \-\-slot\-cores=4 \-\-njobs=200
> done
$ flux schedbench report locality \-\-sort=\-mean_locality_score
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
Benchmark inside a real allocation against real hardware:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
$ flux alloc \-N 4 \-\- flux schedbench run throughput \-\-exec \-\-njobs=500
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
Sweep node counts and schedulers in a single run, with a 1\-node child
per point:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
$ flux alloc \-N 16 \-\- flux schedbench sweep throughput \e
    \-\-nodes=16,64,256,1024 \e
    \-\-scheduler=sched\-simple,sched\-backfill \e
    \-\-njobs=10000
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
Sweep from a TOML file with a fluxion recipe — multi\-module load plus
env overlay:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
$ cat smoke.toml
test = \(dqfill\-machine\(dq
nodes = \(dq16\-1024:2\(dq
njobs = 49152

[[scheduler]]
name = \(dqfluxion\(dq
env = [
  \(dqFLUXION_HOME=/home/user/git/flux\-sched\(dq,
  \(dqFLUX_MODPROBE_PATH_APPEND=$FLUXION_HOME/etc/modprobe\(dq,
]
[[scheduler.modules]]
name = \(dqsched\-fluxion\-resource\(dq
[[scheduler.modules]]
name = \(dqsched\-fluxion\-qmanager\(dq
$ flux start \-s 1 \-\- flux schedbench sweep \-\-from=smoke.toml
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
Pretty\-print results for one benchmark:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
$ flux schedbench report throughput
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
Export results to CSV for downstream plotting:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
$ flux schedbench report throughput \-o csv > throughput.csv
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
Show only runs with a particular tag, sorted by throughput descending:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
$ flux schedbench report throughput \-\-filter=tag=large\-throughput \e
    \-\-sort=\-throughput
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
Show all runs from a single sweep, sorted by node count:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
$ flux schedbench report fill\-machine \-\-filter=sweep.name=smoke \e
    \-\-sort=nodes
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.SH FILES
.INDENT 0.0
.TP
.B \fB\&./schedbench\-results.json\fP
Default results file written by \fBflux schedbench run\fP and read
by \fBflux schedbench report\fP\&. Override with
\fB\-\-results\-file\fP\&.
.TP
.B \fB~/.config/flux/flux\-schedbench\-report\-TEST.toml\fP
Per\-benchmark user\-defined output formats consumed by
\fBflux schedbench report \-o FORMAT\fP\&. See \X'tty: link https://flux-framework.readthedocs.io/projects/flux-core/en/latest/man5/flux-config.html'\fI\%flux\-config(5)\fP\X'tty: link'
for general format syntax.
.UNINDENT
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.INDENT 0.0
.TP
.B FLUX_SCHEDBENCH_ISOLATED
Set to \fB1\fP by \fBflux schedbench run\fP when launching the inner
schedbench invocation inside the fake\-resources subinstance. The inner
invocation checks this variable as a recursion guard and runs the
benchmark directly rather than launching another subinstance. Users
should not normally set this.
.UNINDENT
